MLLM description: The red-backed sandpiper is a small shorebird with a slender body, long legs, and a slightly upturned bill. Its plumage is primarily brownish-gray with darker streaks and spots, especially on the back and wings. The underparts are pale gray, and the head has a dark cap and a white supercilium. The legs are long and thin, and the bill is straight and pointed. The red-backed sandpiper is often seen wading in shallow water or walking along the shoreline, probing the mud or sand with its bill to search for food. It is a migratory species, breeding in the northern parts of Europe and Asia and wintering in Africa and southern Asia. The red-backed sandpiper can be distinguished from other similar species by its distinctive color pattern, size, and behavior.
